An enterprise manufacturing network requires an architecture that prevents corporate firewalls, deep packet inspection (DPI), or strict egress rules from blocking operational traffic.

Hosting the frontend on **Cloudflare Pages**, the API/edge logic on **Cloudflare Workers**, the database on **Supabase (PostgreSQL with RLS)**, and offloading all messaging (WhatsApp, SMS, Email) to **Cloudflare Queues and asynchronous backend micro-tasks** eliminates client-side egress dependencies.

---

### Manufacturing Plant Firewall Immunity Architecture

Plant corporate firewalls frequently block third-party API domains (e.g., Twilio, Meta Graph API, SendGrid) and drop non-standard ports (like direct database connection poolers on port `5432` / `6543`).

```
[ Plant Network (Behind Strict Corporate Firewall / DPI) ]
   |
   | (Port 443 HTTPS only to enterprise custom domain: transit.plant.com)
   v
[ Cloudflare Edge Layer ]
   ├── Cloudflare Pages (Frontend Web App & Static Assets)
   └── Cloudflare Workers (Backend API Router)
            │
            ├──── [1. Real-time DB Queries] ───> Supabase (via HTTPS REST / GraphQL API)
            │                                    (No raw TCP port 5432 needed)
            │
            └──── [2. Asynchronous Events] ─────> Cloudflare Queue (Event Message Bus)
                                                        │
                                                        v
                                                 [ Worker Consumer ] (External Datacenter)
                                                        │
                                                        ├─> WhatsApp Business Cloud API
                                                        ├─> SMS Gateway (DLT HTTP API)
                                                        └─> Transactional Email (Resend/SendGrid)

```

#### Firewall Hardening Mechanisms

1. **Single Allowed Domain & Port 443 Only:** The browser and mobile app only communicate with your primary custom domain (e.g., `[https://transit.plant.com](https://transit.plant.com)`). The client never directly calls WhatsApp, SMS, or Supabase endpoints.
2. **Zero Client-Side Messaging Triggers:** All notification dispatches are pushed into an asynchronous queue (`Cloudflare Queues`) directly from the backend worker upon booking approval or QR check-in. The worker consumer runs entirely within Cloudflare's global cloud network, bypassing all plant perimeter restrictions.
3. **Database Egress Resilience:** Instead of establishing raw TCP sessions on port `5432` (frequently blocked by internal plant proxies), Workers connect to Supabase over standard HTTPS port `443` via PostgREST / Supabase JS client with service-role authentication.

